  Human-readable summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
  Full legal code:        https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode

The full legal code at that URL is the licence. The summary below is not a
substitute for it; it states plainly what this licence requires of you.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
YOU ARE FREE TO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Share    — copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format,
             including by making it available over a network on demand.
  Adapt    — remix, transform, and build upon the material.

For any purpose, including commercially. These freedoms cannot be revoked so
long as you follow the terms below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
UNDER THE FOLLOWING TERMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. ATTRIBUTION. You must give appropriate credit, provide a link to the
   licence, and indicate if changes were made.

   The attribution line to reproduce, matching what v0.1 actually contains:

     Zhongdex by SayMei — https://zhongdex.org — CC BY-SA 4.0.
     Contains data from CC-CEDICT (CC BY-SA 4.0) and the HSK 3.0 (2026)
     word list.

   ../NOTICE is always the current source list; later releases add to it.
   Keep the per-record attribution that ships with the data: every record
   carries `sourceIds`, and every gloss carries its own `source` and
   `license`. When sentence records ship, Tatoeba rows will carry their
   upstream id the same way; that field IS the attribution for those rows and
   must survive your transformation.

2. SHAREALIKE. If you remix, transform, or build upon the material, you must
   distribute your contributions under CC BY-SA 4.0 or a
   ShareAlike-compatible licence.

   MIT and Apache-2.0 are NOT on Creative Commons' compatible-licence list.
   You cannot relicense this data under them.

   ShareAlike propagates through a merge: a database containing a substantial
   portion of this data is Adapted Material under §4 (sui generis database
   rights) and must itself be BY-SA.

   "Share" is defined by the licence as making the material available such
   that members of the public may access it from a place and at a time
   individually chosen by them. That definition covers an HTTP API and an MCP
   server exactly as it covers a download.

3. NO ADDITIONAL RESTRICTIONS. You may not apply legal terms or technological
   measures that legally restrict others from doing anything the licence
   permits.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THIS LICENCE, AND NOT A PERMISSIVE ONE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is not a preference. Every gloss in the v0.1 canon comes from CC-CEDICT,
which is CC BY-SA 4.0. ShareAlike propagates through the merge, so BY-SA is the
only licence this corpus can carry. Wiktionary senses and Tatoeba sentences are
planned and neither changes the outcome. The code that builds it is MIT
(see ../LICENSE); the corpus cannot be.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS NOT COVERED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * The audio recordings. They are served from a separate host under their
    own terms, are referenced by URL and never bundled here, and AS OF v0.1
    ARE NOT PUBLISHED AT ALL — every audio field reports status "pending".
    See the README.
  * SayMei trademarks, the SayMei and Zhongdex names, and the logos. Licence
    terms do not grant trademark rights.
  * The build code in ../src and ../scripts, which is MIT.
  * ../scripts/cedict.json, which is ALSO CC BY-SA 4.0 but is governed by
    ../NOTICE §1a rather than by this file, because it is a verbatim
    redistribution of CC-CEDICT rather than part of the derived corpus. The
    licence is the same; the "changes were made" statement is not.

